Analyzing failures:

1. **"a reply matching by rootCommentId counts as targeted"** — expected 0 unaddressed, got 1. My test: reply `{ threadId: null, rootCommentId: 5550001, reply: 'fixed' }` and thread `ghThread('PRRT_a', 5550001)` — threadKeyOf(t) = threadId 'PRRT_a' (thread HAS threadId!). The reply targets 'c5550001'. Key mismatch: thread key uses threadId first, reply key set has c5550001 (rootCommentId) — threadKeyOf(thread) = 'PRRT_a' not in targetedKeys → unaddressed. 

The matching must be bidirectional: thread matches a reply if the reply's threadId == thread.threadId OR reply's rootCommentId/inReplyToId == thread.rootCommentId. My key-set approach only works when both sides use the same id kind. Fix buildReplyCoverage to do per-thread matching:

```js
var unaddressed = open.filter(function(t) {
    return !(replies || []).some(function(r) {
        if (!r) return false;
        if (t.threadId && r.threadId && String(t.threadId) === String(r.threadId)) return true;
        if (t.rootCommentId !== null && t.rootCommentId !== undefined &&
            ((r.rootCommentId !== null && r.rootCommentId !== undefined && String(t.rootCommentId) === String(r.rootCommentId)) ||
             (r.inReplyToId !== null && r.inReplyToId !== undefined && String(t.rootCommentId) === String(r.inReplyToId)))) return true;
        return false;
    });
```
Wait but GraalJS — Array.prototype.some exists in GraalJS (ES2020). The repo uses filter/map/forEach; some() should be fine. But to be safe use a plain loop or filter with boolean. I'll use filter with a matches function using indexOf-style loop... some() is fine (existing repo code uses `.some(...)` — yes, in test files `loaded.jiraMoveToStatusCalls.some(...)` and production code? grep shows `.some(` used in js files: pushReworkChanges? Let me just use it — GraalJS supports ES6 array methods.

Hmm wait — but then threadKeySet/threadKeyOf still needed for the accounting block. For accounting, matching live thread → inputKeys: same bidirectional problem! Live thread {threadId, rootCommentId} vs input thread {threadId, rootCommentId} — if the live fetch returns both ids (it does), key = threadId matches. Drift case: live thread not input at all → correct. But an input thread matched only by rootCommentId (threadId missing input, present live)? Edge — threadKeySet approach: input thread {rootCommentId: 555, threadId: null} → key 'c555'; live {threadId: 'PRRT_x', rootCommentId: 555} → key 'PRRT_x' → mismatch → labeled drift. To be robust, the accounting classifier should check membership by EITHER id: 

```js
function threadMatchesAnyOf(t, set) {
    // set has keys from threadKeySet — check both id kinds
    if (t.threadId && set[String(t.threadId)]) return true;
    if (t.rootCommentId !== null && t.rootCommentId !== undefined && set['c' + t.rootCommentId]) return true;
    return false;
}
```
threadKeySet already adds both keys per thread ('PRRT_a' and 'c1'). So threadMatchesAnyOf(t, inputKeys) checks t.threadId OR t.rootCommentId against the set — handles mixed id availability. Use that in buildOpenThreadAccountingBlock.

For the "no PR → []" test: `fetchLiveOpenThreads(loaded.scm, null, inputThreads)` — my implementation: `if (!scm || typeof scm.fetchDiscussions !== 'function' || !pr || !pr.number)` → fallback = inputThreads filtered → returns [PRRT_a]. But my test expected [] for no-PR. Hmm — which is right? If there's no PR, we can't verify; falling back to the input snapshot (1 open thread) is the honest fallback — the comment would then list 1 open thread (truthful per last known state) rather than claiming clean. My test asserted [] ("cannot claim anything open, but also nothing cached") — that's actually the WRONG expectation for honesty; the snapshot fallback is more honest. Fix the TEST: expect ['PRRT_a'] for no-PR fallback. The prUrl etc. all come separately. Yes — test bug. Change expectation.

2. **"one LOUD line per unaddressed thread"** — expected 3 lines got 2. The test: coverage built from threads [PRRT_a (targeted), PRRT_b] — coverage.unaddressed = [PRRT_b] → summary + 1 per-thread = 2 lines. My test expected 3 — because I thought unaddressed=2? Let me re-read the test:

```js
var coverage = loaded.mod.buildReplyCoverage(
    [ghThread('PRRT_a', 1), ghThread('PRRT_b', 2, {...})],
    [{ threadId: 'PRRT_a', reply: 'one' }]
);
var lines = loaded.mod.logReplyCoverageGap(coverage);
assert.equal(lines.length, 3, 'summary + one line per unaddressed thread');
